#{[17592186533912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533912 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533912 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533912 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533912 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b697133a-5977-5733-b1c6-65040dd5324d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533948 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533948 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533948 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533948 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533948 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533948 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533948 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533948 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533948 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ecec1431-9990-543a-b776-c6ce2fb531c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533950 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533950 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533950 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533950 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533950 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533950 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533950 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533950 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533950 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efdeec04-664e-562e-b615-6cc0242e52ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533983 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +1){x}^{-\\theta -2},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $1.5,\\,1.8,\\,2.0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b3b7675-fd44-5cc4-931a-4915684e1c8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533983 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +1){x}^{-\\theta -2},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +1){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2} \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1.5\\cdot 1.8\\cdot 2=5.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +1)^{3}(5.4)^{-\\theta -2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534002 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534002 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534002 #edb/ref 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ad2c176-6641-542e-87e9-2e557825f205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534002 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}-4}{16-2(7)+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4}{16-14-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-y}-4)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{2x-y-16}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{16-2x+y}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\cancel{16-2x+y}}{\\cancel{(16-2x+y)}(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4} & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{8}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534020 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N\\},\\,B=\\{3n∣n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=\\{1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},…\\}$\n\nII. $A\\cap B=∅$\n\nIII. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7429a73e-417e-56af-b923-b010e58f428c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534020 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\{3n∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{3,6,9,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\nA\\cap B=\\{1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},…\\}\\cap \\{3,6,9,…\\}=∅.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534037 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534037 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534037 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534037 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $P(x,y):$ $x+y<1$\n\nII. $Q(z):$ $\\frac{6}{z}=8$\n\nIII. $R(n):$ $-n\\in N$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534037 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9db48f80-e375-5a5e-b7bd-dd584b0ae10d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534037 #edb/ref 1074 "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $z$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534051 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534051 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534051 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534051 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/15/155e2eac58e35942918ef52682ebfdad4df283409372ac4a54d20218c363704e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534051 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b213b7e1-d060-5456-8c0f-13babec4bbc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534051 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/r